Prospero based coherence test (base vs optimized)

This folder contains a small, directed test harness for exercising the MESI L1 coherence logic using SST's prospero.prosperoCPU traces.

The harness runs the same build twice:
  - base: coherence_opt = 0
  - optimized: coherence_opt = 1 (controlled by an environment variable)

------------------------------------------------------------
Files and their roles

example-4core-prospero.py
  Builds the SST model:
    - 4 x prospero.prosperoCPU (one per core)
    - 4 x private L1 caches using memHierarchy.Cache (MESI protocol)
    - 1 shared bus (memHierarchy.Bus)
    - 1 shared L2 cache (memHierarchy.Cache)
    - 1 memory controller (memHierarchy.MemController + memHierarchy.simpleMem)

  It also:
    - loads per-core trace files from PROSPERO_TRACE_DIR via prospero.ProsperoTextTraceReader
    - sets coherence_opt on each L1 using OPT_ON

run-prospero-traces.py
  Wrapper that runs the SST model twice:
    1. runs base mode (OPT_ON=0)
    2. runs optimized mode (OPT_ON=1)

  The script writes output files with names like:
    - prospero_mesi_base.out
    - prospero_mesi_opt.out

prospero-traces/prospero_core{0..3}.trace
  Default per-core trace inputs consumed by prospero.ProsperoTextTraceReader.
  To test a new directed scenario, edit these trace files.

------------------------------------------------------------
How to run

From this folder:
  python3 run-prospero-traces.py

Note: you do not set OPT_ON manually; the runner sets it for base vs optimized runs.

------------------------------------------------------------
Trace file format (Prospero text trace)

Each line in prospero_core*.trace has the form:
  <cycle> <op> <addr> <lenBytes>

Where:
  - <cycle>: Minimum cycle count at which the request can be issued
  - <op>: operation, e.g. R (read) or W (write)
  - <addr>: Mem address
  - <lenBytes>: access size in bytes

------------------------------------------------------------
How to change the directed test (editing traces)

1. Decide the access pattern you want to compare (base vs optimized). Common options:
   - all cores repeatedly read the same line
   - a round-robin read-then-write to the same line

2. Edit prospero-traces/prospero_core0.trace ... prospero-traces/prospero_core3.trace so each core performs the desired sequence on the target address(es).

3. Rerun:
   python3 run-prospero-traces.py

If you prefer not to modify the same traces, create a new directory with the same filenames (prospero_core0.trace ... prospero_core3.trace) and run:
  PROSPERO_TRACE_DIR=/path/to/your-traces python3 run-prospero-traces.py


